---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout title="Vedolux 50 with Storage Tanks | Smokeless Heat Blog" description="How the Vedolux 50 wood gasification boiler pairs with thermal storage tanks for efficient, convenient heating.">
  <Header />

  <main class="pt-24">
    <section class="py-16 bg-charcoal text-cream">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6">
        <p class="text-ember text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.3em] mb-4">Installation</p>
        <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl md:text-5xl">Vedolux 50 with Storage Tanks</h1>
      </div>
    </section>

    <article class="py-16 bg-cream">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 space-y-6 text-charcoal/90 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The Vedolux 50 is a wood gasification boiler designed to work with thermal storage (accumulator) tanks. Pairing the boiler with storage tanks allows for batch burning—fully burning a load of wood at high efficiency and storing the excess heat for later use.
        </p>

        <p>
          Without storage tanks, a wood boiler must often throttle back or idle when the house load is low. That leads to lower efficiency, more smoke, and more frequent tending. With properly sized storage tanks, the Vedolux 50 can run at full output, charge the tanks, and then shut down while the house draws heat from storage.
        </p>

        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-darkwood mt-10">Why Storage Tanks Matter</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li>They allow the boiler to burn a full load to completion instead of smoldering.</li>
          <li>They store excess heat produced during a burn for use later in the day or overnight.</li>
          <li>They reduce the number of times you need to light the boiler.</li>
          <li>They help maintain better stratification when the system is properly piped and pumped.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-darkwood mt-10">Typical System Approach</h2>
        <p>
          The Vedolux 50 charges one or more insulated storage tanks through a dedicated boiler loop. A loading unit (such as a Laddomat or similar) is often used to protect the boiler from low return temperatures and to help maintain stratification in the tanks. The house heating zones then draw from the top of the tanks via a mixing valve or secondary circulators.
        </p>

        <p>
          Tank sizing depends on the heat load of the house and the output of the boiler. Larger homes or those in colder climates generally benefit from more storage volume so that a single burn can carry the house longer between firings.
        </p>

        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-darkwood mt-10">Benefits in Practice</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li>Cleaner burns with less smoke</li>
          <li>Higher overall efficiency</li>
          <li>Fewer firings per day</li>
          <li>More consistent house temperatures</li>
          <li>Ability to use stored heat even after the fire is out</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="mt-8">
          When sized and piped correctly, a Vedolux 50 with thermal storage tanks provides a practical and efficient way to heat with wood while minimizing the time spent tending the fire.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
